# simpl_agent_sandbox integration guide

> **Maintained by**: the simpl_agent_sandbox repo, auto-synced to simpl_knowledge.
> **Source of truth**: `simpl-techs/simpl_agent_sandbox/.agent/SKILL.md`

**Status: experimental.** It runs on a personal Vercel Hobby account with test data
only. Not for production data until SimpL's own Vercel Pro team and a DPA covering
Sandbox exist. Plan: `simpL/plans/agent-sandbox-plan.md`.

## What this library is

Short-lived VMs for one task each, behind a `SandboxProvider` protocol. The code box
runs with no network at all; the browser box reaches public sites only, never a
private, loopback, link-local or other non-public address.
Providers: `vercel` (Vercel Sandbox over httpx, no SDK) and `fake` (in memory, for
tests). The package takes bytes in and gives bytes out; storage stays with the caller.
It moved here from `simpl_core.sandbox` (`simpl-techs/simpl_core#335`).

## Installation

```toml
# pyproject.toml of the consuming repo; internal git deps follow main
"simpl-agent-sandbox @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_agent_sandbox.git@main"
```

Its only runtime dependency is httpx. The task runners need the `tasks` extra
(simpl_ia with its agents, simpl_tracker): `simpl-agent-sandbox[tasks] @ ...`.

## Basic usage (the 90% case)

```python
from simpl_agent_sandbox import NetworkPolicy, SandboxConfig, SandboxFile, SandboxSpec
from simpl_agent_sandbox.vercel import VercelSandboxProvider

config = SandboxConfig.from_env()  # SANDBOX_VERCEL_TOKEN, _TEAM_ID, _PROJECT_ID required
async with VercelSandboxProvider(config.vercel) as provider:
    spec = SandboxSpec(
        image=config.workbench_image,
        network=NetworkPolicy.deny_all(),  # any other policy needs its switch, below
        region=config.region,
        timeout_s=600,  # Vercel stops the sandbox then, even if this process dies
        tags={"task": "press-kit"},
    )
    session = await provider.create(spec)
    try:
        await provider.write_files(session, [SandboxFile("/workspace/in/a.csv", data)])
        result = await provider.run(session, ["python3", "job.py"], timeout_s=120, cwd="/workspace")
        outputs = await provider.read_files(session, ["/workspace/out/report.pdf"])
    finally:
        usage = await provider.destroy(session)  # record it with simpl_tracker
```

## Rules and conventions

- There is no default network policy, region or timeout. A provider declares what it
  enforces in `capabilities()` and raises `SandboxUnsupportedError` for anything else,
  before creating a sandbox; it never falls back to a weaker policy.
- The Vercel provider offers a policy other than `deny_all` only when the host turned
  on its switch: `public_internet` with `VercelSandboxConfig.allow_public_internet`
  (`SANDBOX_VERCEL_ALLOW_PUBLIC_INTERNET=true`), `sites` with `allow_sites`
  (`SANDBOX_VERCEL_ALLOW_SITES=true`). Both are off by default, so a caller cannot
  open the network by code alone. Only the browser box asks for `public_internet`.
- `NetworkPolicy.public_internet()` reaches every public IPv4 address and nothing in
  `NON_PUBLIC_IPV4_NETWORKS` (private, loopback, link-local with the cloud metadata
  address `169.254.169.254`, carrier-grade NAT, multicast, reserved), and no IPv6.
  Vercel's firewall checks the address a connection actually goes to, after DNS.
- `NetworkPolicy.only_sites` takes bare domains (subdomains included) and refuses IPs,
  wildcards and shared front ends such as `googleapis.com` or `cloudfront.net`, which
  would let the sandbox reach other tenants by domain fronting.
- `run` takes an argv (no shell unless you run one), streams output to `on_output`, and
  returns a `CommandResult` with each stream cut to `output_limit_chars` (head and tail).
  A non-zero exit is a result; `timed_out` marks a command killed at `timeout_s`.
  `on_output` gets every byte uncut: never forward it to a model or store it as is.
- `read_files` maps a missing file to None. Errors are the `simpl_agent_sandbox.errors`
  classes only; no Vercel type, id or error leaves the adapter (`SandboxSession.provider_ref`
  is opaque: never log or store it).
- `destroy` returns a `SandboxUsage` (run time, active CPU when known, vCPUs, memory,
  region, our sandbox name), or None when the sandbox was already gone. Its fields match
  `simpl_tracker.sandbox.SandboxUsage`: the caller records it; this package never does.
- `sweep(tag_key=..., tag_value=..., older_than=...)` destroys only non-persistent
  sandboxes carrying that tag.
- Hosts build each provider from config and register it once at startup in
  `SandboxProviderRegistry`; a policy names the provider to use.
- The browser image carries the T0 path-A pieces in `/opt/sbx`: start `browserd.py
  [--no-downloads] [--typed] [--read-checks=N]` detached (Chromium plus an in-browser
  request filter), then run
  `act.py '<json action>'` once per step (`step`: unfreeze the page, one action, settle,
  snapshot, freeze; the step's `screenshot` saves a PNG among the downloads; plus
  `navigate`, `snapshot`, `downloads`). The filter lets through
  only GET, HEAD and OPTIONS to public addresses, checked after DNS (loopback and the
  CDP port included, which the firewall cannot see), in every tab, popup, frame and
  worker, plus page script's reads by POST from any public host that is not refused,
  until anything is typed (D83 widened by G25, below),
  and browserd removes `WebSocket`, `WebSocketStream`, `WebTransport` and
  `RTCPeerConnection` from every page, frame and worker, because WebSockets never reach
  the filter. `act.py` refuses to act when the daemon is not running, refuses any click,
  typing or download on an element that sends a form, and its JSON line stays under
  12,000 characters. The browse runner below drives them.
- Images: `images/{workbench,browser}/`, tags pinned in `config.py` as the content hash
  of each folder. The code box (workbench) has Python 3.12 with pandas, duckdb, pillow,
  openpyxl, xlsxwriter, xlrd, python-docx, python-pptx, reportlab, weasyprint, pypdf,
  pdfplumber, matplotlib, markdown, jinja2, qrcode, cairosvg, beautifulsoup4,
  charset-normalizer, tabulate and pyyaml (exact pins in
  `images/workbench/requirements.txt`), plus headless Chromium, ffmpeg, poppler-utils,
  pandoc (no LaTeX: PDFs through `--pdf-engine=weasyprint`), qpdf, jq, zip and unzip;
  the helper's prompt lists the same. Code checks still prove a file's shape only: an
  `.xlsx`, `.docx` or `.pptx` output is checked with `FileExists` or `FileSize`.
- Building: CI (`.github/workflows/workbench-image.yml`) builds, pushes and probes the
  workbench when its folder changes; the pull request carries the new pin. On `main`,
  after the probe passes, CI points the moving tag `simpl-sbx-workbench:stable` at that
  image (registry copy, no rebuild); a manual run with `tag_stable=<content tag>` rolls
  it back after running the probe's isolation checks on that image (not its tools
  check). A rolled-back box may lack tools the current prompt names. A host that sets
  `SANDBOX_WORKBENCH_IMAGE=simpl-sbx-workbench:stable` gets each merged image on its next
  sandbox with no library release or setting change: Vercel resolves the tag at create
  time (checked live 2026-11-02). The tag pinned in `config.py` stays the default. The
  helper's prompt names new packages only from the library version that added them.
  By hand (the browser box): `python -m simpl_agent_sandbox.images.build <image>
  --project <vercel project>`, then run the probe `tests/test_conformance_probe.py`
  (`SANDBOX_LIVE=1`) before using a new tag.

## Task runners (`simpl_agent_sandbox.tasks`)

Two task-level runners (D4): each starts one sandbox, runs a DeepSeek sub-agent inside
it with its own low-level tools, and destroys the sandbox. This package imports
simpl_ia's agents and pydantic-ai: install the library with the `tasks` extra
(`simpl-agent-sandbox[tasks] @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_agent_sandbox.git@main`).

```python
from simpl_agent_sandbox.tasks import (
    CsvCheck,
    DownloadCompleted,
    PdfCheck,
    StructuredClientModel,
    TaskBudget,
    TaskInput,
    deepseek_client,
    run_browse_task,
    run_code_task,
)

# The host passes the key; one model per task groups its receipts under one agent_run_id.
model = StructuredClientModel(deepseek_client(deepseek_key), process_name="ops.marketing.code_task")
result = await run_code_task(
    "Turn the press kit into a one-page A4 brief ...",
    [TaskInput("simpl-press-kit.zip", zip_bytes)],  # staged into /workspace/in
    [
        PdfCheck("/workspace/out/press-brief.pdf", pages=1, page_size_pt=(595, 842)),
        CsvCheck("/workspace/out/inventory.csv", rows=7, columns=("path", "bytes")),
    ],
    TaskBudget(max_steps=30, wall_clock_s=600),
    provider,
    model=model,
    tags={"task": "press-brief"},
    on_usage=record_usage,  # (sandbox_id, SandboxUsage | None, destroy error | None), T7
)
result.outcome, result.files, result.steps, result.usage

# The provider must allow the public internet: VercelSandboxProvider built with
# allow_public_internet=True (SANDBOX_VERCEL_ALLOW_PUBLIC_INTERNET=true); otherwise
# create() refuses before any request.
result = await run_browse_task(
    "Open the Press page and download the press kit.",
    "https://www.simplsales.ai/",  # start URL: public http(s) only
    TaskBudget(max_steps=20, wall_clock_s=300),
    provider,
    [DownloadCompleted("simpl-press-kit.zip")],
    allow_downloads=True,
    model=StructuredClientModel(
        deepseek_client(deepseek_key), process_name="ops.finance.browse_task"
    ),
)
result.answer.text  # UntrustedText: the sub-agent read pages nobody checked
```

- **No network for code, the public web for browse**: `run_code_task` creates the
  workbench with `NetworkPolicy.deny_all()`; no argument changes it, and the provider
  deletes a sandbox Vercel did not create that way. `run_browse_task` takes a public
  start URL and creates the browser box with `NetworkPolicy.public_internet()`: the
  sub-agent may open any public site (D28). The Vercel provider refuses that policy,
  before any request, unless the host turned on `allow_public_internet`, so a host that
  never sets it can run code tasks only.
- **One action per model turn**, through native tool calling: each tool is a
  pydantic-ai output tool, so the model answers with exactly one tool call, our code
  performs it and the next turn starts from a fresh prompt. Code tools: `write_file`,
  `run`, `read_file`, `done`. Browse tools: `click`, `type`, `select`, `scroll`,
  `navigate` (public http(s) URLs only), `download` (only with `allow_downloads`), `wait`,
  `done`; never page script or raw CDP.
- **Model calls** go through simpl_ia's `StructuredLLMClient`, built from a typed
  `ModelPolicy` plus a credential binding (`deepseek_client`, default `deepseek-flash`),
  and run inside an `LLMAccountingScope(process_name=..., agent_run_id=...)`, so every
  request leaves a simpl_tracker receipt. Pass your own `SubAgentModel` to swap models.
- **Caps** (`TaskBudget`): steps (default 30, at most 60), wall clock (default 600 s;
  the sandbox lives exactly this long, D5), a timeout on every model call and command,
  and output files per file, in total and by count. A file over a cap stays in the
  sandbox and is listed in `skipped_files`; checked files are read first, and `done`
  is refused while a checked file is over a cap (the refusal names the cap). Every
  call after the loop is cut to the sandbox's remaining life; a read that fails then
  becomes a skipped file. When it is a checked file, the outcome is `OUTPUT_MISSING`
  instead of `DONE`, and `detail` names the checked files that did not come back.
- **Loop guard**: the same action with the same effect twice in the last 6 steps puts a
  warning in the next prompt; a third time ends the task with `LOOP_STOPPED`.
- **Done gate**: `done` is accepted only when the caller's checks pass, otherwise
  refused with the failing checks named. A code task that runs out of steps or time
  still ends `DONE` when the final checks pass and every checked file comes back. Code
  checks run inside the workbench from a script passed on the command line, each on a
  file directly in `/workspace/out/` (the only files that come back): `FileExists`,
  `FileSize`, `PdfCheck` (pages, page size), `ImageCheck` (pixels), `VideoCheck`
  (duration, frame size), `CsvCheck` (rows, columns), `JsonKeysCheck` (exact or
  contained keys). Browse goal checks: `DownloadCompleted` (a finished download) and
  `PageAt(host, path_prefix)` (the open page's parsed URL: that host exactly, under
  that path by whole segments, query and fragment ignored, loaded with HTTP status 200
  to 399) are evidence, and a browse task needs at least one of them.
  `PageUrlContains` (text anywhere in the URL, so the sub-agent can write it into a
  query or fragment) and `AnswerContains` (the sub-agent's own answer) are format
  checks only. `PageAt` proves a page loaded there, not what it says: a site that
  answers 200 for any path passes for one that does not exist.
- **Untrusted content**: everything a sandbox printed, every page and the browse step
  results (they quote the page) are fenced in the prompt with a fresh mark per prompt,
  and every page field is on one line. `CodeTaskResult.summary` and
  `BrowseTaskResult.answer` are `UntrustedText`; output files and downloads are
  untrusted bytes to store, never to run or render on a server.
- **Results**: `outcome` (`TaskOutcome`: done, output_missing, submit_asked, bot_check,
  step_cap,
  time_cap, loop_stopped, model_failed, sandbox_failed, browser_failed) with `detail`, the files,
  the check verdicts, the step log (`StepRecord`: action, short detail and result, model
  and sandbox time), `model_usage` (requests and tokens), `usage` (`SandboxUsage` from
  `destroy()`) and `image_digest`, the exact image the sandbox ran (`sha256:...`, as
  Vercel resolved the tag; also on `SandboxSession` and in the `sandbox.created` and
  `sandbox.task.*_finished` log lines; `None` when the provider reports none). `DONE` means the checks passed and every checked file or
  `DownloadCompleted` download came back; `output_missing` names the ones that did not.
  The step log is returned, never stored here (D7). A browse result also has
  `blocked_requests` (how many requests browserd blocked) and `blocked_top`: its 10
  largest groups as `BlockedRequests` (host, method, reason, count; the host comes from
  the page, so untrusted). When the browser stopped, `detail` holds browserd's exit line
  (reason and code), including the first one after a restart that let the task go on.
- **Errors**: a failed `create` raises `SandboxError` (nothing ran). After that, sandbox,
  browser and model failures are outcomes; an unexpected error raises `TaskRunError`
  with the step log and token count so far; a cancellation is re-raised. In every case
  the sandbox is destroyed in a shielded task that then calls `on_usage` (a `UsageSink`),
  so the cost reaches the caller even after a cancellation. `cleanup_error` says when
  the destroy failed (logged with the sandbox id); the provider timeout still ends it.
  A sink that raises is logged and never replaces the result. Before the host shuts
  down, it awaits `drain_destroys()`, so destroys whose callers were cancelled finish;
  a destroy cancelled anyway reports `(sandbox_id, None, "CancelledError")`.
- **Browse safety** (without a submit context: reads and downloads public pages; typing
  is for site search):
  the sandbox reaches public IPv4 addresses only, checked by the firewall after DNS;
  browserd's filter passes only GET, HEAD and OPTIONS to public addresses (a name is
  refused unless every address it resolves to is public; loopback and the CDP port
  included), and browserd removes the WebSocket, WebTransport and
  WebRTC constructors from every page, frame and worker. One exception (phase 7, D83):
  with no submit permit open, a POST passes when page script sent it (XHR or Fetch,
  never a document, beacon or CSP report), to any public host that is not on the
  refused list (G25), before anything was typed in the task, with a
  readable body of at most 64 KB and no personal-data or card shape, at most 300 bodies
  checked per task (passed or not). Sites such as HubSpot load their prices that way. A GET, HEAD or OPTIONS with a
  body goes through the same check. `result.read_posts` counts them; nothing is logged. Our code refuses any click,
  typing or download on an element that sends a form (a submit or image input, a submit
  button, or anything inside one) before it reaches the browser, and act.py refuses it
  again at click time. Typed text loses its line breaks, so Enter is never pressed. URLs
  with a backslash, whitespace, control characters or credentials are refused. Not
  covered: page script that sends typed text in a GET request to a site (a search box
  does exactly that).
- **What the sub-agent reads** (phase 7, G9-G11): the page's text on the screen, then
  below it (1,500 characters), each control's state after its name
  (`(on)`, `(checked)`, `(expanded)`), and its own notes from earlier steps (each page
  action may carry `notes`, at most 1,500 characters, the latest kept), fenced apart
  from the page. Two actions read the whole page without scrolling, on demand (G12):
  `find(text)` (controls with refs and text lines holding the words, about 2,000
  characters) and `read_more` (the next 4,000 characters below what was seen). Near a
  cap each prompt says to finish (G19), and a task the loop guard or the step cap stops
  gets one last turn that may only call `done` (G20): `DONE` when its checks pass.
  Done is refused once when a listing task has not read its page to the end (G17), and
  once when the answer gives amounts or counts that no page of the task showed (G16);
  a second done goes, with those amounts in `result.unverified`; a figure the task text
  itself quotes counts as seen, and a currency amount must match a currency amount the
  pages printed, a count a count (G35). A listing task about prices or plans has done
  refused once while the answer leaves out a price the task's own site printed (G34:
  "these prices were on the page but are not in your answer: $446 ..."); a second done
  goes, with those still left out in `result.missing_prices`. A pricing task (monthly, yearly, billing) on a page with
  a billing switch has done refused once until the sub-agent used the switch (G27); a
  second done goes, and `result.detail` ends "answered without using the page's billing
  switch ...". A click on a switch, tab or box adds what it reads now to the step's
  result. A regional redirect (zendesk.com to zendesk.de, de.example.com, /de/) is named
  in the step and never counts as a loop (G18); another host or path of the site
  (app.example.com, /go/pricing) is not one. A bot check on the open page (its title or
  first text, or a refused request to a CAPTCHA service's host and path beside almost no
  text) ends the task `BOT_CHECK`, with fixed words in `detail` (G26).
- **Screenshots as files** (phase 7, D87): with `allow_downloads`, the sub-agent also
  gets `screenshot(name, full_page=False)`. The PNG (the screen, or the page down to
  6,000 px and 1,280 px wide; at most 2 MB, else at half scale, else refused; a name
  already saved is refused) is saved among the
  downloads under that name and comes back in `files` like a download, so a
  `DownloadCompleted("plans.png")` goal checks it and the host's type check (D19) keeps
  it. Its description says to use it only when the task asks for a picture.
- **Form submits** (phase 4, opt-in): pass `submits=SubmitContext(switch, origin,
  private)` to `run_browse_task`. The host sets it, never the model: `switch` is
  `OPS_BROWSE_SUBMITS` (`off` behaves exactly as no context), `origin` is `owner` or
  `tainted`, `private` whether the task holds private data. The sub-agent then gets
  `submit` (on a SUBMIT element) and `type` with `enter` (in a one-line text field).
  Each press is decided by fixed rules from facts the page cannot lower: free submits
  go under browserd's one-request permit, which checks the body at the wire; a submit
  the owner must approve ends the task `SUBMIT_ASKED` with `result.ask` (`SubmitAsk`:
  the page, the form's fields and exact values, the rules, a PNG screenshot); refused
  ones (accounts, logins and codes, payments, CAPTCHAs, posting on social and comment
  sites) press nothing. `result.submits` holds a `SubmitRecord` per try, for the
  audit: values only for free submits. After the owner's press, the host calls
  `run_form_submit(ask, provider, ...)`: a new box, no model, the approved values
  filled, sent only when the form still hashes as the owner saw it; the outcome is
  `executed`, `not_sent`, `failed` or `unknown` (pressed, no answer: never send it
  again).
- **Open-web risks** (D28, accepted by Jackie): a page can trick the sub-agent into
  opening a URL that carries text from its context, the task included, to any site;
  link-click actions (unsubscribe, confirm or sign-in links) can fire on any site; the
  requests come from Vercel under Jackie's personal Hobby account, meant for personal
  non-commercial use, so heavy scraping can get it flagged and stop code tasks too;
  heavy pages spend Hobby's 5 CPU-hours a month faster; and text a page planted can ride
  in `answer`, which is why it is `UntrustedText`. Offer `browse_task` only where its
  context holds nothing private (D26: through helper profiles).
- **Browser driver** (D16): the runner talks to a `BrowserDriver`. `CommandBrowserDriver`
  (the `command` driver) runs browserd detached and one act.py command per step; the
  page stays frozen while the model thinks. A navigation without a load event within
  `nav_timeout_s` (15 s) is retried once in a new tab that browserd opens; on browserd's
  exit line Chromium is restarted once in the same sandbox and the last page reopened,
  and a second exit ends the task with `BROWSER_FAILED`. It counts blocked requests by
  host, method and reason in memory (at most 200 groups; more hosts count as one), and
  the sub-agent's next page shows a `BLOCKED since your last action: 14 × POST
  app.example.com (method)` line, at most 5 groups, only when something new was blocked
  (D84). A `cdp_websocket` driver is planned behind the same interface.
- **Live view (developers only)**: `scripts/live_view.py` runs one task and serves its
  step log and the browser's frames on `http://127.0.0.1:8791/`. Pass a `TaskObserver`
  subclass to `run_*_task` to see events yourself; what it receives is raw and must
  never reach a model or `tool_call_runs`.

## Host package (`simpl_agent_sandbox.host`)

What a host runs inside one `code_task` or `browse_task` tool call (0.3.0; moved
from simpl_core's agent framework, plan `sandbox-host-out-of-core-plan.md` M1, with
the same names). The host registers its own tools; inside each call it builds a
`SandboxToolContext` and awaits a run. Needs the `tasks` extra.

```python
from simpl_agent_sandbox.host import (
    BrowseTaskSpec,  # the tool arguments the model fills in (pydantic)
    CodeTaskSpec,
    SandboxPolicy,
    SandboxToolContext,
    SandboxToolHost,
    SandboxUsageReport,
    SubmitTaskFacts,
    run_browse_tasks,
    run_code_tasks,
    run_form_submits,
    structured_client_sub_agents,
)

host = SandboxToolHost(  # once, at startup
    sub_agent_model=structured_client_sub_agents(deepseek_client(key)),
    file_store=store,  # SandboxFileStore protocol; None: no inputs, outputs not kept
    on_usage=record_usage,  # SandboxUsageReport -> simpl_tracker; None: a tracking gap
    on_ask=stage_ask,  # async (whole ask) -> staged id | None; only for form submits
    browser_image=image,  # region and images default to the pinned ones
)
context = SandboxToolContext(  # per call, never from the model
    host=host,
    policy=SandboxPolicy(provider="vercel", tools=["browse_task"], allow_downloads=True),
    process_name="ops_sandbox:agent-slug",
    attribution={"user_id": "...", "pipeline_run_id": "...", "node_run_id": "..."},
    deadline=deadline,  # time.monotonic() instant: the earliest of the host's limits
    submit_facts=SubmitTaskFacts("owner", private=False),  # browse with submits only
)
reply = await run_browse_tasks(context, tasks)  # dict the calling model reads
```

- **One call is a batch** (D17): `policy.parallel` tasks at once (an
  `asyncio.Semaphore`), at most `MAX_SANDBOXES_PER_PROCESS` (8) sandboxes across the
  process and `MAX_BROWSERS_PER_SITE` (2) browsers starting on one site. One failing
  task never stops the others; results come back in input order, each with its
  `index`, `outcome`, `done` and `detail`. A cancellation of the call is raised.
- **Time**: each task gets its share of the time left before `deadline`, less
  `FINISH_MARGIN_S` (30 s) for the model to read the reply; a call that would give a
  task less than 120 s is refused before any sandbox starts. The host computes the
  deadline: its turn's end, and the model call's cut if it has one (simpl_core read
  that from its own context variable; this package never imports simpl_core).
- **Costs**: every sandbox's usage goes to `on_usage` as a `SandboxUsageReport` with
  the context's `process_name` and `attribution` as they are; put `node_run_id` and
  `pipeline_run_id` in the attribution to group costs by run. A failing sink is
  logged and reported as a tracking gap, never raised.
- **Files**: code task inputs are loaded by reference from the file store; outputs
  and downloads are saved there, and the row lists each with its `ref` or why it was
  `not_kept`.
- **What the model reads**: every text is cut to a fixed size; page- or
  sandbox-derived text sits under `untrusted_` keys (`untrusted_summary`,
  `untrusted_answer`, `untrusted_final_url`). A browse row also has `blocked_requests`,
  `blocked_top` (the 10 largest groups of blocked requests: `untrusted_host`, `method`,
  `reason`, `count`; D84; a method that is not HTTP's reads `OTHER`) and
  `unverified_amounts` (amounts or counts in the answer
  that no page of the task showed, kept after one refused done; G16) and
  `missing_prices` (prices the task's own site printed that a listing answer still
  left out; G34). Refusals (a
  non-public start URL, downloads off, too many tasks, a tool the policy does not
  offer, two tasks on one start page, D86) come back as rows or a `refused` / `error`
  key, not exceptions. A create the provider refuses for lack of room (Vercel's 429,
  `SandboxUnavailableError` with `reason` `capacity` or `rate_limited`) waits about 15 s,
  doubling to 60 s, and tries again while the task would still get its minimum time
  before the deadline (3 minutes in all with none); then its row is `sandbox_unavailable`
  with `detail` "no sandbox was free, so this task did not run: try it again later",
  then the provider's words (G28).
- **Form submits** (phase 4): with `policy.submits` set to `ask` or `on`, browse tasks
  run under the submit rules with the host's `submit_facts` (None: tainted and
  private). Each row lists its `submits`; a task that ends `submit_asked` hands the
  whole ask to `on_ask` (with the form's screenshot kept in the file store) and the
  model reads only the staged id, the rules and the page's host. After the owner
  approves, `run_form_submits(context, [staged_ask])` presses it in a new box with
  no model; `ask_from_row` rebuilds and rechecks a staged ask, raising `ValueError`
  for one the rules would now refuse.
- **Logs, not alerts**: warnings on the `simpl_agent_sandbox.host.*` loggers
  (`sandbox.host.usage_sink_failed`, `output_not_kept`, `task_not_started`,
  `task_failed`, `task_crashed`, `ask_not_staged`, `screenshot_not_kept`), each with
  the sandbox id or task index, `doing` and `cause` as `extra` fields and the
  exception as `exc_info`; `task_refused` and `task_finished` at info. The library
  sends no notification: to alert on these, add a `logging.Handler` on the
  `simpl_agent_sandbox.host` logger that forwards WARNING records to your notifier
  (for simpl_tracker: `LOG.warning(record.getMessage(), notify="warning",
  error=record.exc_info[1], **extra_fields)`).
- **Tests**: replace the runners where the host package calls them,
  `simpl_agent_sandbox.host.runs.run_code_task` / `run_browse_task` /
  `run_form_submit`, and register the fake provider.

## Testing

Use the fake provider; it refuses exactly what Vercel refuses.

```python
from simpl_agent_sandbox import NetworkPolicy, SandboxSpec
from simpl_agent_sandbox.fake import FakeOutcome, FakeSandboxProvider


async def handler(sandbox, argv):
    return FakeOutcome(stdout="done\n")


provider = FakeSandboxProvider(handler=handler)
session = await provider.create(
    SandboxSpec(image="any", network=NetworkPolicy.deny_all(), region="fra1", timeout_s=60)
)
result = await provider.run(session, ["python3", "job.py"], timeout_s=10)
```

## What this library does NOT do

- It is not `simpl_sandbox`, the local cross-repo dev stack with a local Supabase.
- It holds no storage: bytes go in and bytes come out; the caller stores files.
- It records no costs: `destroy` returns usage for the caller to record with simpl_tracker.
- It registers no agent tools: a host (simpl_ops) builds its own `code_task` and
  `browse_task` around `simpl_agent_sandbox.host`, and decides who may call them.
- It never imports simpl_core.

